On 16 November 2026 Google said Gemini Notebook can now turn the sources in a notebook into an interactive site. This post goes through what Google's announcement, an earlier post about slides and videos, and the Gemini Notebook Help Center say, with a focus on how you would start one, who can use it and the questions Google's pages leave open. Everything about the product comes from those three Google pages.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Google is a competitor of Anthropic, so this is not an independent view from a neutral party. Stackbrook did not test any product mentioned and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What Google announced

The announcement, written by a Google Labs software engineer, describes Notebook generating interactive sites from a notebook's sources to help with understanding complex material. It gives three example uses: a visual explainer for exam prep (the post mentions an interactive periodic table or a historical timeline), an interactive dashboard for exploring data such as quarterly sales performance, and a project tracker that lays out tasks and deadlines.

On availability, Google says the feature is rolling out globally on the web for Gemini Notebook users aged 18 and over, including Google Workspace subscribers, and is coming soon to the mobile app. No date is given for mobile. The page does not name countries, languages, plan tiers or prices, and it does not say when the rollout will finish.

## How to start one

The steps in the post are short. Open your chat in Gemini Notebook, type "make me a site" followed by a description of what you want to build for work or study, and when the site is ready choose "Open in a new tab" to use it. The post also says the site can be shared with others.

What the post leaves out is just as useful to know. It does not explain how to pick which sources the site draws on, whether you can edit a site after it is built, what sharing permissions apply, or what happens to a shared site later. It states no usage caps and no caveats about accuracy, privacy or data handling. Google's pages do not document these points.

