## How it compares with slides and videos

Gemini Notebook already produced other outputs from sources, and Google's other pages describe them in more detail. They are useful because they show how Google expects people to steer a generated result.

In a post from 19 October 2026, Google describes added creative control for Slide Decks and Video Overviews. You ask in the chat for slides or a video, Notebook suggests topics based on your sources, and once you confirm you can guide content, visual style and format. A review toggle lets you look at a plan first, called outline notes for slides and producer notes for video. You can add feedback, such as an executive summary at the start or a different scene order, before anything is rendered, and then choose Proceed. Google says the toggle can be turned off to generate directly from the Studio panel.

The Help Center article on slide decks adds practical detail. You can start from the Studio panel, choose a format, output language and length, and describe the deck; generation can take several minutes while you keep working. A finished deck can be revised slide by slide with a Revise option, and each revision creates an entirely new deck. The article says there is no limit on how many individual slides you revise, but there are quota limits on the total number of decks you can revise, with no numbers given. Decks can be downloaded as PDF or PowerPoint files, and the article carries a warning that slide decks are AI-generated and may contain visual or factual inaccuracies. It also states that sources are not taken into account during revisions.

The interactive sites post describes none of these controls. Based on the published text, a site is created from a single typed request, with no outline step mentioned. Whether sites will gain the review or revision options that slides have is not stated anywhere in the pages read.

## Sharing and access details that exist today

The sites announcement does not describe sharing rules; the details below come from the slide article only. For decks, a share link requires the notebook to be shared with the recipient or set to anyone with a link, and recipients then have access to the full notebook. The article says public notebook sharing is only for consumer accounts and is disabled for Workspace Enterprise or Education accounts. It also says deleting a deck breaks earlier share links, and that generating or deleting a deck needs edit access to the notebook. The slide article gives an age limit of over 18, while the sites announcement says aged 18 and over.

If you work in a Workspace organization, the announcement says Workspace subscribers are included, while the slide article says public notebook sharing is disabled for Workspace Enterprise and Education accounts. Google does not say how that applies to sites.

## What is not confirmed

Several things cannot be answered from Google's pages: whether any plan or country is excluded, whether there are limits on the number or size of sites, how sources are chosen, whether sites can be revised, how sharing works for sites, what the privacy position is for shared sites, and when the mobile app will support them. The Help Center article read here covers slide decks only, and a separate help page for video overviews was not read. This post is general information about a vendor announcement; it is not a product test and it does not recommend using or avoiding the feature.
